#text("Construction, 2026-11-08, T-1939. The Round 60 norm primitive now supports an implemented orbit-representative method with explicit transporter matrices and scalar recovery. Across five frozen or amended small-curve panels, all 63 targets recover: nine through nontrivial collisions, nineteen through order-three stabilizers and thirty-five through known-value orbit equations. Five tautological attempts are retained and restarted under the frozen rule. This is demonstrated on the declared cases, not a mixing, runtime or reliability claim. All scientific statements remain unsigned producer derivations or exact checks. [pcc:unverified]")
#parbreak()
#text("Marked action and representatives. For W=[z]G the candidate evaluates T(W)=[1/(1-z)]G with the complete transverse norm. S(W)=G-W encodes 1-z. The six S 3 images are z,Tz,T^2z and their complements. Choose the lexicographically least point encoding and retain its normalized 2x 2 transporter. A generic canonicalization needs two norm targets; each target-dependent graph constructor is charged. If T fixes a point, the second call is reused. Public special values and the projective infinity tag are handled separately; finite scalar 0 is the group identity. The reference backend uses known scalar labels and is explicitly separate from the candidate. [pcc:unverified]")
#parbreak()
#text("Closed scalar tracking. A walk state keeps f_M(k)=(a*k+b)/(c*k+d). Both canonicalization and known affine moves update M by left multiplication, so the rational degree stays 1. The frozen moves are z+1,2z+3,3z+5,5z+7, chosen from an FNV 1a hash of the encoded state and restart index. Adding the original unknown target to a generic Mobius state would raise the degree and is excluded. The walk has four declared restart offsets and a cap of r+2 stages per attempt. All inputs, moves, failures and stopping rules were filed before affected outputs. [pcc:unverified]")
#parbreak()
#text("Scalar collision equation. Equal finite encoded points from M 1,M 2 yield(a 1*c 2-a 2*c 1)k^2+(a 1*d 2+b 1*c 2-a 2*d 1-b 2*c 1)k+(b 1*d 2-b 2*d 1)=0. Projectively proportional matrices produce the zero polynomial and are rejected. A nontrivial collision gives a linear or quadratic equation; Tonelli-Shanks and public point verification select the scalar. Known-value and order-three stabilizer orbits also give explicit equations, recorded separately in every trace. orbit-proof.md contains the algebra, exception handling and cost argument. [pcc:unverified]")
#parbreak()
#text("Complete orbit checks. The program qualifies all 444 projective scalar states across group orders 7,19,199,17,197, checking 2664 transformed point/matrix pairs. It retains every representative and transporter. There are 81 orbits in total: three size 2,ten size 3 and 68 size 6. The free-orbit rearrangement check verifies 816 identities H(Sz)*S=H(z) and H(Tz)*T=H(z). These are deliberately tautological comparisons: an orbit identity alone does not give a scalar. Genuine walk collisions require nonproportional accumulated transporters. [pcc:unverified]")
#parbreak()
#text("Recovery paths, not an aggregate oracle win. The original r 7 andr 19 panels recover largely through exceptional orbits; the r 199 panel contributes six genuine collisions. Exact P-192 arithmetic shows its scalar field is 2mod 3, with no order-three stabilizer roots. Amendment 1 therefore adds the r 17 twist control; amendment 2 adds a larger matched control selected solely by a frozen point-count rule. It chooses E/F 211:y^2=x^3-3x+5 of prime order 197 after retaining the earlier rejected counts. All eleven targets there recover, including three genuine collisions without the order-three shortcut. The amendments preserve the original data and make no unbiased performance comparison. [pcc:unverified]")
